course: religion-ethics
lesson: 5
title: Joseph and his brothers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Jacob loved his son Joseph most and gave him a richly decorated coat; Joseph also told his brothers about dreams in which they bowed down to him, and their jealousy grew.
explain: The brothers threw Joseph into a pit, sold him to traders and told their father a wild animal had killed him; in Egypt Joseph refused to do wrong with his master Potiphar's wife, was falsely accused and put in prison.
explain: In prison he helped others and explained dreams, and when he explained Pharaoh's dreams of seven fat and seven thin cows, Pharaoh put him in charge of storing grain, and Joseph saved Egypt and many nations from famine.
explain: When his brothers came to buy grain and bowed before him, Joseph tested whether they had changed, then revealed himself, wept and forgave them, saying "You meant it for evil, but God meant it for good" (Genesis 50:20).
explain: For a child today: favouritism hurts, being right is not the same as speaking wisely, one wrong leads to bigger ones, and you can do good even in a hard place.
explain: Above all, forgiveness is stronger than revenge, and Christians believe God can bring good even out of the bad things people do.
fact: Joseph was one of the youngest of Jacob's twelve sons.
fact: Jacob loved Joseph most and gave him a richly decorated coat, which made his brothers jealous.
fact: Joseph dreamed that his brothers' sheaves of corn, and the sun, moon and stars, bowed down to him, and he told them.
fact: The brothers threw Joseph into a pit, sold him to traders and told their father he had been killed by an animal.
fact: In Egypt Joseph refused to do wrong with his master Potiphar's wife; she falsely accused him and he was put in prison.
fact: In prison Joseph helped the other prisoners and explained the dreams of Pharaoh's baker and cupbearer.
fact: Pharaoh dreamed of seven fat cows and seven thin cows; Joseph explained this meant seven years of plenty, then seven of famine.
fact: Pharaoh put Joseph in charge of storing grain, and he saved Egypt and many nations from famine.
fact: The brothers came to Egypt for grain and bowed before Joseph without knowing him; he tested whether they had changed.
fact: Joseph revealed himself, wept and forgave his brothers.
fact: Joseph told his brothers: "You meant it for evil, but God meant it for good" (Genesis 50:20).
fact: Christians believe God can bring good even out of bad things people do; this does not make the bad things right.
fact: Genesis, with Joseph's story, is part of the Jewish Torah as well as the Christian Bible.
fact: The word "coat of many colours" comes from old English Bibles; newer ones often say "richly ornamented robe".
exercise: Why were Joseph's brothers jealous of his coat? | Their father loved Joseph most and gave only him the special coat | The coat had been stolen from them | Joseph had bought it with their money | It was too small for any of them
exercise: What does the story of Joseph's coat teach parents and teachers? | Favouritism causes hurt, so they should be fair | Children should all wear exactly the same clothes | Bright colours always make other people jealous | Only the youngest child should ever get presents
exercise: What did Joseph dream about? | His brothers' sheaves, and the sun, moon and stars, bowing down to him | A ladder to heaven with angels on it | Seven fat cows and seven thin cows | A rainbow over a great flood
exercise: What lesson comes from Joseph telling his brothers his dreams? | Being right is not the same as being wise in how we speak | You should always say everything you think | Dreams should be kept secret from everyone forever, even from your family | Brothers never want to hear good news
exercise: What did the brothers tell their father about Joseph? | That he had been killed by an animal | That he had run away to live in Egypt | That he had gone off to become a king | That he was lost somewhere in the desert
exercise: How does the story of Joseph being sold show how wrongs grow? | Jealousy led to cruelty and then to lying | It shows that small wrongs always stay small and go away | It shows lying fixes the first wrong | It shows jealousy never leads anywhere
exercise: Why was Joseph put in prison in Egypt? | His master's wife falsely accused him after he refused to do wrong | He stole grain from Pharaoh's stores | He tried to run back home | He told Pharaoh a bad dream
exercise: What does Joseph's refusal in Potiphar's house teach? | Doing right can cost us something, yet integrity is worth it | Doing right always brings an instant reward | It is fine to do something wrong if no one is there to see it | Honest people never get into trouble
exercise: What did Joseph do in prison? | He helped other prisoners and explained their dreams | He planned his revenge on his brothers for the day he got out | He slept and did nothing for years | He tried to escape every night
exercise: What does Joseph in prison teach us? | We can do good wherever we are, even in hard places | You should only help others when you are happy yourself | Prison is a good place to be | People in hard places cannot do anything good
exercise: What did Pharaoh's dreams of fat and thin cows mean? | Seven years of plenty, then seven years of famine | Seven years of war, followed by seven years of peace | Egypt would soon have more fat cows than ever before | Pharaoh himself would soon become very ill and die
exercise: What wisdom does the story of Pharaoh's dreams teach? | Plan ahead and save in good times | Spend everything while you have it | Never think about the future | Dreams always come true exactly
exercise: What job did Pharaoh give Joseph? | Storing grain and running Egypt to save it from famine | Looking after Pharaoh's cows | Building pyramids for Pharaoh | Guarding the royal prison
exercise: What does Joseph's rule in Egypt teach about gifts and power? | They are given to serve others | They are given to make yourself rich | They should be used to punish enemies | They are only for kings and queens
exercise: Why did Joseph test his brothers when they came for grain? | To see whether they had really changed | To get his revenge for all that they had done to him | To make them pay more for the grain | Because he did not recognise them
exercise: What does Joseph's test teach about trust? | It is fair to look for real change before trusting again | You should never trust anyone who has hurt you, not ever again | You should trust everyone straight away | Tests are a way to get revenge
exercise: What did Joseph do when he revealed himself to his brothers? | He wept and forgave them | He sent them to prison | He made them his servants | He told Pharaoh to punish them
exercise: What does "You meant it for evil, God meant it for good" mean? | God can bring good even out of bad things people do | What the brothers did was actually good | Evil things never really happen, they only seem bad to us | People should do evil so good can come
test: my brother says Joseph got the coat cos he was the oldest. right? | No, he was one of the youngest; his father just loved him most | Yes, the oldest always got the coat | No, he bought it in Egypt | Yes, and his brothers were all younger
test: Mum always lets my sister pick the film and never me, and I'm fuming. Which story warns parents about this? | Joseph's coat: favouritism causes hurt | Noah's ark: God keeps his promises | The Tower of Babel: pride divides | Creation: God rested on the seventh day
test: Ben came top in the test and keeps telling everyone "I'm smarter than all of you lot." Like Joseph with his dreams, what's the problem? | Being right isn't the same as being wise in how you speak | He should hide that he came top so that nobody else gets jealous | Coming top is always wrong | It's fine because it's true
test: Your friend broke a window, then lied to his dad, then blamed his little brother. Which part of Joseph's story is like this? | The brothers: one wrong led to bigger wrongs | Joseph forgiving his brothers when they came to Egypt | Pharaoh's dreams of the seven fat and thin cows | Joseph helping other prisoners while in prison
test: Grace, who is Jewish, says, "Joseph's story is in our Torah too." What is right? | Yes, Genesis is part of the Jewish Torah and the Christian Bible | No, Joseph is only in the Christian Bible | The Torah has a different Joseph | Christians took the story out of the Torah and changed it completely
test: Uncle Dan says: "Joseph was lucky, he just fell on his feet." What does the story say Joseph did, even in prison? | He kept doing good and helping others | He waited in his cell and did nothing at all for years | He bribed the guards to let him out | He kept quiet and hid his gifts
test: Your dad says "Save some of your birthday money, don't blow it all." Which part of Joseph's story fits? | Storing grain in the years of plenty for the famine | Joseph's brothers selling him | Joseph telling his dreams | The brothers bowing to Joseph
test: Your older brother pushed you around all last year, now he's being nice. "Don't trust him, he's faking," says a friend. What does Joseph's story suggest? | Look for real change, and be ready to trust again | Never trust him again, whatever he does, because people never change | Get him back first, then trust him | Pretend nothing ever happened
test: Mila says, "Joseph's brothers did a good thing in the end, because Joseph saved Egypt." What is wrong with this? | What they did was still wrong; God brought good out of it | Nothing, the brothers did a good thing | The brothers had planned all along for Joseph to go and save Egypt | Joseph never saved Egypt in the story
test: Olympiad judge: "Joseph had power over his brothers. Why was forgiving them wiser than revenge?" | Forgiveness restored the family; revenge would have broken it for good | Because he was too busy to punish them | Because revenge is not allowed in Egypt | Because they had not done anything wrong
test: Judge's follow-up: "Was Joseph right to refuse his master's wife, even though it sent him to prison?" | Yes, integrity is worth it even when doing right costs something | No, he should have done whatever she wanted so that he would stay safe | No, he should have run away from Egypt | Yes, because he knew he would become a ruler
exam: Identify the principal cause of the enmity borne towards Joseph by his brothers, as related in Genesis. | Jealousy occasioned by their father's favouritism and by Joseph's dreams | A dispute concerning the division of their father's flocks | The theft by Joseph of the grain belonging to his brothers and their father | An offence committed by Joseph against the Pharaoh of Egypt
exam: Ascertain the interpretation given by Joseph to the dreams of Pharaoh concerning the cattle. | Seven years of abundance to be followed by seven years of famine | Seven years of warfare to be followed by seven years of peace | The impending death of Pharaoh and the succession of his eldest son | The multiplication of the herds of Egypt beyond number
exam: Select the response most consistent with the declaration recorded in Genesis 50:20. | Good may be brought about by God even from deeds intended as evil | Deeds intended as evil are thereby rendered good | No wrong was in fact committed by the brothers | Evil deeds are to be repaid with evil in equal measure
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of Joseph upon revealing himself to his brothers? | He wept and extended forgiveness to them | He caused them to be imprisoned forthwith | He required them to serve him as bondsmen | He demanded restitution for the wrongs he suffered
